// Practice quiz: static members and methods
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/methods-and-recursion/static-members-and-methods

::o1 calling through the class::A class Geometry declares static double circleArea(double r). How does code in an unrelated class, one that does not extend Geometry and has no static import, call it? {
~circleArea(2.0), with no class name#Not quite. Inside Geometry the class name may be left out, but this unrelated class has no inheritance or static import that would let the bare name resolve.
~geometry.circleArea(2.0), with a lowercase g#Not quite. Java names are case-sensitive, and no variable called geometry exists.
=Geometry.circleArea(2.0)#Correct. A static method belongs to the class, so you call it through the class name.
~Geometry.main first, and then circleArea(2.0)#Not quite. A static method needs no setup call; the class name is enough.
}

::o1 why main is static::If a traditional main were not static, what would the launcher need before it could call main? {
~Nothing different; static only makes main run faster#Not quite. static is about belonging to the class, not about speed.
~A second main method to call the first one#Not quite. The launcher calls one main method directly.
~Permission for other classes to see main#Not quite. Visibility comes from public or private, not from static.
=An object of the class, because an instance method runs on an object#Correct. A static main needs no object, which is why the classic form is static.
}

::o2 shared total::A class has static int total \= 0 and a method static void add(int n) whose body is total +\= n. After add(2) and then add(5), what is total? {
~5#Not quite. total is not reset by each call; the 2 from the first call is still there.
=7#Correct. There is one shared total, so each call adds to what the earlier calls left.
~2#Not quite. The second call also adds to the same shared field.
~0#Not quite. Both calls change the one static field.
}

::o2 fresh every call::Which variable starts again from its initial value every time a method is called? {
~One declared static at the top of the class#Not quite. A static field has one copy that keeps its value between calls.
~Any variable of type int#Not quite. The type does not decide this; where it is declared does.
=One declared inside the method body#Correct. A local variable belongs to one call and disappears when that call returns.
~Any variable whose name is in capital letters#Not quite. Naming style has no effect on how long a variable lives.
}

::o3 reaching an instance field::A class Player has the instance field int lives \= 3, and its static main prints lives directly. How do you make main print a lives value? {
~Add return lives at the end of main#Not quite. main is void, and the problem is that no Player receiver is available to read lives from, not a missing return.
=Create an object, Player p \= new Player(), and print p.lives#Correct. An instance field belongs to an object, so static code reaches it through one.
~Print this.lives#Not quite. A static method has no this, because it runs without any particular object.
~Print Player.lives#Not quite. Player.lives would work only if lives were a static field.
}

::o3 no object::Why can a static method not read an instance field directly? {
=It runs without any particular object, and every object has its own copy of the field#Correct. Without a receiver object, there is no particular copy of the field for the method to read.
~Instance fields are always private#Not quite. Access modifiers are a separate rule; the issue is which object's copy to read.
~Static methods cannot read any fields at all#Not quite. They can read static fields directly.
~Instance fields do not exist until main finishes#Not quite. Instance fields exist as soon as an object is created with new.
}
